# DTF tier manifest: token-colon-scope  (#2989 / 1.7.0)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The scope-vocabulary mismatch this tier
# proves is storage-independent — the broken check is the pure scope match in
# `scopes_grant_access`, ahead of any storage backend.
#
# The bug (#2989): repo-scoped tokens minted via POST /repositories/{key}/tokens
# carry COLON-FORM scopes (`write:artifacts`, `read:artifacts`, ...) but the
# artifact upload/mutation handlers enforced the BARE `write` scope via
# `require_scope("write")`. The vocabularies never matched, so a legitimate
# least-privilege `write:artifacts` repo-scoped token got 403 on upload (fails
# closed — broken flow, not a hole).
#
# The fix: handlers require the resource-specific `write:artifacts`, and the
# central matcher gains ONE new rule — a held BARE parent satisfies a
# colon-form requirement (held `write` covers required `write:artifacts`).
# Direction is broad-covers-specific ONLY: held `write:artifacts` satisfies
# neither bare `write` (settings-class handlers) nor another resource's scope.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (MAIN)     repo-scoped `write:artifacts` token -> PUT artifact 201.
#              RED on the pre-fix baseline (403 "required scope: write"),
#              GREEN on the fix. A DB assert confirms the artifact row exists.
#   (CONTROL)  personal bare `write` token -> PUT artifact 2xx on BOTH images
#              (bare-parent rule keeps the legacy vocabulary working).
#   (BOUNDARY) the same `write:artifacts` token -> PATCH /repositories/:key
#              (settings write, bare `write` gate) -> 403 on BOTH images;
#              -> upload to a DIFFERENT repo -> denied (repo binding intact);
#              repo-scoped `read:artifacts` token -> upload 403 on BOTH images.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login + user create +
# user login + three token mints + several back-to-back uploads; the base
# default (ON) would throttle them (same rationale as 5f2q-upload-scope).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
